# No Code-Built UI — Prefab-Authored Pipeline

The committed **prefab is the single source of truth** for every screen, modal, and overlay. Code that generates UI, or sets its layout at play time, competes with the prefab and silently destroys hand-authored edits. These five rules are non-negotiable, wherever UI code happens to live in a project.

## 1. Edit prefabs, not runtime code

UI layout (positions, sizes, anchors, hierarchy) is authored on the `.prefab` in the Unity Editor or via Unity MCP. Runtime view scripts only update **content and state**. Designers tune prefabs in the Inspector; hardcoded layout in C# overrides those edits at play time.

```csharp
// ❌ BAD — play-mode layout overrides prefab edits
rowRt.anchorMin = rowRt.anchorMax = new Vector2(0.5f, 0.5f);
rowRt.anchoredPosition = new Vector2(RowCenterX, y);
rowRt.sizeDelta = new Vector2(RowWidth, RowHeight);

// ✅ GOOD — layout authored on prefab; code updates content
[SerializeField] private TextMeshProUGUI _amountText;
public void SetAmount(string text) => _amountText.text = text;
```

**Banned in runtime UI code**

- `anchoredPosition`, `sizeDelta`, `anchorMin`/`anchorMax`, `offsetMin`/`offsetMax`, `pivot`, `localPosition`/`localScale` on UI chrome for baseline placement
- Layout constants (`RowWidth`, `FirstRowY`, `NameFontSize`, …) used to position or size elements
- `new GameObject(...)` + `AddComponent<Image|Button|TextMeshProUGUI|LayoutGroup>` to build screen chrome
- **Static text.** A label that never changes at runtime ("Leave Game?", button captions, headers) is set on the prefab, not via `.text = "..."` in `Awake` — code re-assigning it overrides prefab edits exactly like hardcoded layout

**Allowed at runtime**

- **Content/state:** `.text` for **data-driven** values (scores, names, timers), `.color`, sprite/material swaps, `SetActive`, `interactable`, toggles. State-driven literal selection (`"YOU WIN!"` vs `"YOU LOSE"`) is content — mark it with the allow comment
- **Data-proportional sizing:** fill width/height from a **serialized** authored max (stat bar fill = `_barWidth * ratio`) — track and max live on the prefab
- **Animations:** temporary transforms during tweens; restore or let the animation end
- **Safe area:** the one dedicated safe-area component (see the ui-safe-area rule) — not per-screen offsets
- **Layout rebuild after content change:** `LayoutGroup` / `ContentSizeFitter` on the prefab; code may call `LayoutRebuilder.ForceRebuildLayoutImmediate` when dynamic text changes size
- **Pooled/list rows:** instantiate a **row prefab** authored in Editor; bind data only — no per-row layout math

A genuinely justified line carries `// layout-guard: allow — reason` on the same or previous line. `Editor/` scripts and one-off prefab migration tools may set `RectTransform` values — that is authoring, not a play-mode override.

## 2. Prefab edit recipe (Unity MCP)

`instantiate_prefab` into the open scene → inspect with `get_hierarchy` / `get_components` → edit with `set_rect_transform`, `set_ui_text`, `set_ui_image`, `update_component`, `add_ui_element` → `apply_prefab_overrides` → delete the scene instance. Editing an existing instance in the project's UI harness scene works the same way: edit, then `apply_prefab_overrides`.

## 3. No UI builder scripts

Never create editor scripts that generate screen/modal/overlay prefab hierarchy from C# (`Build*Screen*`, `Build*Modal*`, `Build*Overlay*`, `*ScreenAssets*`). Once a prefab exists, a builder script is a second source of truth that overwrites prefab edits when rerun. Harness/scene *assemblers* that only **instantiate** committed prefabs are fine — they can never destroy prefab content.

## 4. One-off tools die in the same change

Any temporary editor script (migration, batch fix, initial generation) is deleted in the same change that commits its output. Recovery is `git revert`, not regeneration. Don't rename a builder around the banned patterns — delete it.

## 5. Screenshot or it didn't happen

A UI change is not done until visually verified: open the project's UI harness/review scene (or the target scene), feed representative data (including stress cases: long names, large values, full lists), take a screenshot, and **read the image** — check positions, overlap, truncation against the design reference. For wired behavior, enter play mode briefly and check the console; views must fail loudly on missing serialized refs, so wiring breaks are visible there. Attach the screenshot to your summary.

**Verification depth is the user's to set, not the agent's.** This is the default when a project says nothing. A project may set a different default in its own `.cursor/rules/` (for example compile-check only); per request, `/no-verify` defers the check to the user and requires a written hand-off — what changed, what to look at, what is unconfirmed — in the screenshot's place, and `/full-verify` forces the whole loop. The check still happens either way; a human does it. Rules 1–4 never relax.

## Rebuild menu items — derived output only

A `Rebuild ...` editor menu item is legitimate **only when its output is derived data nobody hand-edits**: font atlases, asset catalogs/groups, baked textures, generated import settings, dev-only harness scenes. If the output is **authorable** (a prefab, scene content, any Inspector-tuned asset), the rebuilder is a second source of truth — migrate the asset to prefab/Inspector authoring and **delete the rebuilder** (rule 4). Never extend an authored-output rebuilder to add a feature; edit the asset instead.

**Agents run menu items themselves** via Unity MCP (`execute_menu_item`) and verify the console output — never ask the user to run a menu item as a task step.

## Enforcement

Projects enforce 1 and 3 mechanically with a UI-authoring guard (editor script that scans runtime code for the banned members and script names, logs errors on recompile, and fails builds). Pre-existing code-built UI goes in the guard's explicit grandfather list, which only ever shrinks.

## Agent checklist

- [ ] Layout/hierarchy changes made on the prefab (MCP or Editor), not in C#
- [ ] Runtime views only touch content, visibility, and data-driven values; justified exceptions carry `// layout-guard: allow`
- [ ] Dynamic lists use authored row prefabs, not code-built rows
- [ ] No new `Build*Screen*` / `Build*Modal*` / `Build*Overlay*` / `*ScreenAssets*` scripts
- [ ] One-off tools deleted in the same change as their committed output
- [ ] Verification done at the depth the project/user set; screenshot read and attached by default
